lution for all .\n\nConjecture 1.1 has garnered significant interest (cf. [2], [6], [9], [10], [17] and references therein), but remains unresolved in general. While it has been verified for when has small regularity (see [17]) and for random graphs with restricted edge probability (see [4]), progress has been limited. A promising approach to this challenging conjecture is to explore the stronger property of having linear quotients. This property has also been widely studied and, in particular, if a monomial ideal generated in a single degree has linear quotients then it has a linear resolution (cf. [13]). Thus, as observed before, if has linear quotients, for some , then G is necessarily a gapfree graph. We make the following conjecture:\n\nConjecture 1.2\n\nIf has linear quotients for some , then has linear quotients for all .\n\nFor a subset , the induced subgraph {G}_{W} is the graph with vertex set W consisting of all edges where . --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nALGEBRAIC\nCOMBINATORICS\nArindam Banerjee, Selvi Ka (https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/alco.119.pdf)\nciteturn6search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; When G is a gap-free graph and locally of regularity 2, we show that reg Is = 2s ... This is a weaker version of a conjecture of Nevo and Peeva. ... [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 13, 14, 23, 24, 25, 29, 32]). ... Conjecture B (Nevo–Peeva).\nneous ideal I in a standard graded algebra over a ﬁeld, the regularity function reg Is\nis asymptotically a linear function (see also [3, 35]). Though despite much eﬀort from\nmany researchers, this asymptotic linear function is far from being well understood. In\nthis paper, we investigate this regularity function for edge ideals of graphs. We shall\nexplore several classes of graphs for which this regularity function can be explicitly\ndescribed or bounded in terms of combinatorial data of the graphs. This problem has\nbeen studied recently by many authors (cf. [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 13, 14, 23, 24, 25, 29, 32]).\nOur initial motivation for this paper is the general belief that global conclusions\noften could be derived from local information. Particularly, local conditions on an edge\nideal I (i.e. conditions on reg(I : x), for x ∈V (G)) should give a global understanding\nof the function reg Is, for s ⩾1. Our motivation furthermore comes from the following\nconjectures (see [5, 30, 31]), which provide a general upper bound for the regularity\nfunction of edge ideals, and describe a special class of edge ideals whose powers (at\nleast 2) all have linear resolutions.\nConjecture A (Alilooee–Banerjee–Beyarslan–Hà). Let G be a simple graph with\nedge ideal I. For any s ⩾1, we have\nreg Is ⩽2s + reg I −2.\nConjecture B (Nevo–Peeva). Let G be a simple graph with edge ideal I. Suppose\nthat G is gap-free and reg I = 3. Then, for all s ⩾2, we have\nreg Is = 2s.\nManuscript received 7th April 2019, revised 2nd March 2020, accepted 18th March 2020.\nKeywords. Castelnuovo–Mumford regularity, edge ideals, powers of ideals.\nISSN: 2589-5486\nhttp://algebraic-combinatorics.org/--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNevo–Peeva eventual linear-resolution conjecture for gapfree graphs (https://www.emergentmind.com/open-problems/nevo-peeva-eventual-linear-resolution-conjecture-gapfree-graphs)\nciteturn6search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; # Nevo–Peeva eventual linear-resolution conjecture for gapfree graphs ... It then places the authors’ matching-number-three theorem in the context of a broader conjecture attributed to Nevo and Peeva. ... — Edge ideals of gapfree graphs with matching number $3$  (2610.21866 - Erey et al., 15 Oct 2026) in Introduction\n\n# Nevo–Peeva eventual linear-resolution conjecture for gapfree graphs\n\nProve that for every gapfree graph G, all sufficiently large powers of its edge ideal I(G) have linear resolutions.\n\n### Background\n\nThe paper explains that gapfreeness is necessary for powers of an edge ideal to have linear resolutions, but that gapfreeness alone does not guarantee a linear resolution for the square. It then places the authors’ matching-number-three theorem in the context of a broader conjecture attributed to Nevo and Peeva.\n\nThe conjecture remains unresolved in general; the paper establishes it for gapfree graphs with matching number three and, in fact, proves linear quotients for every power beginning with the square in that case.\n\n### References\n\nThey conjectured that it does force $I(G)^{q$} to have a linear resolution for all sufficiently large $q$ Open Problem~1.11(1).\n\n— Edge ideals of gapfree graphs with matching number $3$  (2610.21866 - Erey et al., 15 Oct 2026) in Introduction\n\nTheir stronger regularity conjecture Open Problem~1.11(2) would imply linear resolutions for every $q\\geqreg I(G)-1$.\n\n— Edge ideals of gapfree graphs with matching number $3$  (2610.21866 - Erey et al., 15 Oct 2026) in Introduction\n\nIf $G$ is gapfree with $\\nu(G)=4$, does $I(G)^{s$} have a linear resolution for every $s\\geq2$? Can one prove linear quotients for all these powers?\n\n— Edge ideals of gapfree graphs with matching number $3$  (2610.21866 - Erey et al., 15 Oct 2026) in Question 1, Section Further directions\n\nFor every gapfree graph $G$ with at least one edge, do the following implications hold? \\begin{enumerate}[(a)] \\item If $\\nu(G)\\leq5$, then $I(G)^{s$} has a linear resolution for every $s\\geq2$. \\item If $\\nu(G)\\leq6$, then $I(G)^{s$} has a linear resolution for every $s\\geq3$. \\end{enumerate}\n\n— Edge ideals of gapfree graphs with matching number $3$  (2610.21866 - Erey et al., 15 Oct 2026) in Question 2, Section Further directions\n\n### Related Problems\n\n1.\n\nCombinatorial characterization of all-powers linear quotients  Same Paper\n\n2.\n\nComparison of symbolic and ordinary depths of edge ideals  2610.23224\n\n3.\n\nMonotonicity of the symbolic depth function for edge ideals  