; ## The CAGE (Combinatorics, Algebra, and Geometry) Seminar: Selvi Kara, 8 October 2026 ... Nevo and Peeva conjectured that sufficiently high powers of the edge ideal of a gapfree graph have linear resolutions. Previous work on this conjecture has often imposed additional algebraic hypotheses. ... Building on our previous work, we combine graph-theoretic reductions with operations that preserve linear quotients to reduce the proof to 26 graphs on at most seven vertices.\n## The CAGE (Combinatorics, Algebra, and Geometry) Seminar: Selvi Kara, 8 October 2026\n\n### Gapfree graphs and powers of their edge ideals\n\nSelvi Kara (Bryn Mawr College) | 2026-10-08 at UPenn, David Rittenhouse Laboratory, room 3C8, at 3:30-4:30 PM\n\nAbstract: Powers of edge ideals often reflect the combinatorics of the underlying graph. Nevo and Peeva conjectured that sufficiently high powers of the edge ideal of a gapfree graph have linear resolutions. Previous work on this conjecture has often imposed additional algebraic hypotheses. In this talk, I will discuss an approach based instead on combinatorial restrictions, using the matching number to organize the problem. We focus on gapfree graphs with matching number three and show that every power of their edge ideals starting with the second has linear quotients and hence a linear resolution. Building on our previous work, we combine graph-theoretic reductions with operations that preserve linear quotients to reduce the proof to 26 graphs on at most seven vertices. I will explain these reductions and discuss how this approach may guide further progress on the conjecture. This is joint work with Nursel Erey, Tài Huy Hà, Takayuki Hibi, and Susan Morey.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ncyclic sieving — arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/cyclic%20sieving)\nciteturn6search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; A positivity conjecture for a quotient of $q$-binomial c